This is always the literally billion dollar question that people are asking us.

199
00:15:02,400 --> 00:15:04,800
And you know, I don't think we have any crystal ball here in one more.

200
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:07,920
If Apple makes a change, you are seeing Google start to make some shifts.

201
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:16,080
The I call it pressure from both the EU and a few other regulators, both in state and federal here in the US, is starting to increase.

202
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:22,000
They kind of won the lawsuit against Epic, but listen, a lot of stuff got disclosed and people are starting to dig into it right now.

203
00:15:22,080 --> 00:15:24,000
There's a pretty good lawsuit coming out of the UK.

204
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:28,320
So I believe there's a second case with Google and Epic as well that is unresolved.

205
00:15:28,480 --> 00:15:28,560
Yeah.

206
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:29,040
Yeah.

207
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:33,040
So the question is like at some point do they just say, hey, we're making enough money here.

208
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:34,240
Let's ratchet it back.

209
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:36,960
I think every app developer can ag agree that it's worth something.

210
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:39,840
To be able to sell globally on day one is worth something.

211
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:42,400
And so the question is, is that 15%?

212
00:15:42,560 --> 00:15:43,360
Is it 20%?

213
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:45,520
I ultimately think at some point there will be a crack.

214
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:48,400
And then that is just straight cash flow for the app developer ecosystem.

215
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:58,960
And Apple will find a way to make it back because then those individuals will either lower their prices to get more people to subscribe or they generally increase price over time by adding a lot more features because they're just so much more profitable.

216
00:15:59,120 --> 00:16:03,360
So now they can continue to invest more and more in their businesses because they're generating that much more cash flow.

217
00:16:03,520 --> 00:16:13,760
So I guarantee there's an analysis being done at Apple that, hey, let's figure this out at some point and just kind of shift some of these lawsuits off because you just don't want to be the target of regulators today.

218
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:24,160
Facebook had it, Microsoft has had it at some point that I call it the ISO run of the global regulators will aim at every major technology company and you just want to stay out of that limelight as long as possible.

219
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:29,600
So yeah, it's a bit of tactics at this point, but I almost wonder if even a rate cut is going to be enough.

220
00:16:29,760 --> 00:16:38,080
It's like once a regulator gets the B in their bonnet that they want to crack down on this, I don't think it becomes because it never really was a question of economics, right?

221
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:48,320
I think it's a question of power and sort of making sure that Apple doesn't become bigger and more powerful than these fiat governments, like the DMA right now is happening, Digital Markets Act in Europe.

222
00:16:48,720 --> 00:16:53,280
Did you see the ridiculous cyberpunk press release from the European regulators on this?

223
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:54,720
Oh my God, it's so good.

224
00:16:54,960 --> 00:16:56,320
It's really good in the show notes.

225
00:16:56,480 --> 00:16:59,040
But they like tweeted out who their gatekeepers were.

226
00:16:59,760 --> 00:17:02,880
And for some reason, it's on like a Tron background.

227
00:17:03,200 --> 00:17:04,480
It's like technology.

228
00:17:05,120 --> 00:17:07,120
Anyway, we're living in a very strange timeline.

229
00:17:07,520 --> 00:17:09,040
But I mean, they're already doing it.

230
00:17:09,200 --> 00:17:11,280
And like Apple could cut rates tomorrow, it's over.

231
00:17:11,360 --> 00:17:11,920
It doesn't matter.

232
00:17:12,080 --> 00:17:13,680
Like they're going to fall under these regulations.

233
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:20,560
But it's not over because if Apple does cut rates to compete, they're going to win back a lot of those payments.

234
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:29,680
I think part of what works in your thesis, Eric, is that if Apple lowers rates, they're going to pull back in payments that aren't currently happening on the App Store.

235
00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:45,120
Like this weekend, I was downloading YouTube TV so I could watch a football game and they're one of the few quote reader apps that does let you click a link to go outside the app store and I jumped through all the hurdles and I had to pull out my credit card to get the three-digit.

236
00:17:45,520 --> 00:18:01,280
The Surgeon General's warning against outside apps or Yeah, I had to jump through all the hoops, but somebody like YouTube TV, if it were 10% or something in that ballpark that Apple's charging less, do they want those consumers to be jumping through all these hoops?

237
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:11,520
If there are alternate app stores in Europe, but developers know that they can make more money on Apple's app store if Apple reduces a fee, I hope that's an analysis.

238
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:13,040
that is being done inside Apple too.

239
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:18,320
It's like how much of these payments will we win back if it's ten percent, twelve percent, whatever those numbers are.

240
00:18:18,400 --> 00:18:22,400
I don't think there's enough McKenzie consultants in the world to get that answer right.

241
00:18:22,720 --> 00:18:24,240
Like, it's just too complicated, right?

242
00:18:24,640 --> 00:18:31,520
There's the pricing pressure and there's developer behavior, then there's consumer behavior, then there's conversion rates and throughput on the App Store stuff.

243
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:35,440
I mean, it's like trying to change pricing at a SaaS company, not ask me how I know.

244
00:18:35,680 --> 00:18:39,520
It's like there's just too many levers that it's like impossible to predict.

245
00:18:39,680 --> 00:18:41,520
And at some point, you just have to aim and shoot.

246
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:42,320
I don't know.

247
00:18:42,480 --> 00:18:44,880
I mean, it depends on which side of the demand curve they're on, right?

248
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:47,920
Like, if they cut rates, is that really going to bring people back?

249
00:18:48,240 --> 00:18:50,720
It's a lot of effort to like set up those external systems.

250
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:54,960
So I guess in theory it all equilibrializes in the long game.

251
00:18:55,280 --> 00:18:59,920
But the thing that's got me a little melancholy about it all is it's already a mess.

252
00:19:00,160 --> 00:19:01,280
Like we've already created a mess.

253
00:19:01,520 --> 00:19:06,320
The fact that DNA exists and it will exist forever, it's like another one of these cookie things.

254
00:19:06,640 --> 00:19:10,000
Maybe we're going to get rid of the cookie thing at some point and the internet will be fixed.

255
00:19:10,480 --> 00:19:22,560
But I just worry that this same regulation without fully understanding the consequences that has led to the web being somewhat unusable these days is going to come to apps and it's impossible to predict the consequences.

256
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:23,360
But I don't know.

257
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:24,400
We'll just have to wait and see.

258
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:26,240
I just feel like the cat's out of the bag already.

259
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:27,520
That's on Apple, right?

260
00:19:27,760 --> 00:19:36,480
To take initiative to instead of letting seven seven different jurisdictions create their own laws and then everyone has to jump through it, just take the lead, make a big splash, solve the problem.

261
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:39,520
And I think that's my thesis that they're smart enough.

262
00:19:39,760 --> 00:19:42,480
They will make that decision, I think, when the tradeoff becomes very apparent.

263
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:46,160
If they can solve it, it's already complex against seven different jurisdictions, right?

264
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:49,040
And maybe that was their intent with the first year, fifteen percent.

265
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:54,320
They were trying to take off some of the heat, but it's hard to evaporate twenty billion dollars in EBITDA or whatever that is.

266
00:19:56,240 --> 00:19:58,000
Not for money, but it's about as good as free money.

267
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:00,080
It's about as close as you're going to get, right?

268
00:20:00,800 --> 00:20:10,720
From a regulator's point of view, I do find it really interesting that I don't think Apple and Google get enough credit for just how consumer friendly their payments are you can go to one place to cancel subscribe.

269
00:20:11,520 --> 00:20:12,720
They have fraud protection.

270
00:20:12,960 --> 00:20:23,280
They have all these kinds of layers built in and the regulators better regulating Apple and Google payments directly versus now anyone.

271
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:24,400
Oh yeah.

272
00:20:25,440 --> 00:20:34,080
I mean, there are laws in the US now that are coming up because you have people like the New York Times of all people like making you make a phone call to cancel your subscription.

273
00:20:34,400 --> 00:20:38,960
And so there is from a regulatory standpoint now it's going to be a net consumer loss.

274
00:20:39,280 --> 00:20:39,360
Yeah.

275
00:20:40,080 --> 00:20:40,160
Yeah.

276
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:41,280
Net when it's all done.

277
00:20:41,440 --> 00:20:44,720
I mean, even if the price cuts and whatever, like, I mean, that's always been my stance.

278
00:20:44,960 --> 00:20:47,360
Again, it's hard to give up twenty billion dollars in profit, right?

279
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:51,120
So maybe Apple overplayed their hand by not giving some.

280
00:20:51,920 --> 00:20:57,200
To me, it feels like the market has constructed itself in such a way that it's oligopolized, right?

281
00:20:57,440 --> 00:21:01,920
There's only a handful of players and there's no real way to break that monopoly up.

282
00:21:02,160 --> 00:21:08,240
So now, I don't know, they're trying to create some sort of market pressure, but there's really no way, like you can't replicate a free market.

283
00:21:08,560 --> 00:21:13,840
You know, if it's like IE versus Netscape, okay, at the end of the day, it's an icon you click on.

284
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:20,080
There's sort of like comparable alternatives, but in-app purchases and non-in-app purchases are not the same technology, right?

285
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:27,280
They're not the same thing, but the regulators are trying to act as if that's going to create like sufficiently real market pressure again.

286
00:21:27,440 --> 00:21:29,360
So we've just like half solved anyway.

287
00:21:29,680 --> 00:21:31,040
Complain about European regulations.

288
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:32,720
It's my second favorite activity.

289
00:21:33,440 --> 00:21:34,560
We can run it all up with it.

290
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:35,520
Just stay tuned.

291
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:36,400
Pressure's going.

292
00:21:36,800 --> 00:21:40,480
But it's been like two years of staying tuned and nobody still knows what's going on.

293
00:21:40,720 --> 00:21:44,480
So but I think we will see like when the DMA rolls out, we'll get some hint.

294
00:21:45,120 --> 00:21:46,720
Something will happen in 2024.

295
00:21:47,280 --> 00:21:48,080
Yeah, for sure.

296
00:21:48,720 --> 00:21:54,240
One of the other things you mentioned in the report that I thought was really interesting was you proposed a question.

297
00:21:55,280 --> 00:21:58,160
Who will be the Berkshire Hathaway of App Store?

298
00:21:59,040 --> 00:22:01,280
So who will be Eric?

299
00:22:01,920 --> 00:22:03,600
You posed a question but give me the answer.

300
00:22:03,840 --> 00:22:05,360
And maybe for the finance nerd, explain.

301
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:08,000
what that means or the nonfinance nerds.

302
00:22:08,160 --> 00:22:10,800
Yeah, for everyone who has a social life, I'll explain this.

303
00:22:11,040 --> 00:22:13,120
Berkshire Hathaway is Warren Buffett's company, right?

304
00:22:13,280 --> 00:22:15,360
And he started up just cash flowing businesses, right?

305
00:22:15,520 --> 00:22:20,560
If you look at his portfolio, he's got stuff from like oil and gas pipelines, Sea's Candy, which is chocolate candy.

306
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:22,160
You know, he used to own textiles.

307
00:22:22,400 --> 00:22:24,640
Berkshire Hathaway's, that was the textile company, right?

308
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:25,200
Or something like that.

309
00:22:25,280 --> 00:22:26,400
Exactly, that was the original name, right?

310
00:22:26,560 --> 00:22:27,280
So that was the founding.

311
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:30,560
So basically, Buffett, his thesis was like, he just buys good businesses, period.

312
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:31,040
Right?

313
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:32,240
Doesn't matter what industry.

314
00:22:32,720 --> 00:22:40,560
And so you've seen rollups happen in other industries, car wash industries, you've seen them happen in like doctor's clinics, where people kind of get economies of scale and bring them together.

315
00:22:40,800 --> 00:22:42,640
And you're starting to see that in the Apple store.

316
00:22:42,800 --> 00:22:48,880
But the interesting thing is we haven't seen it to the extent we would have expected given the amount of dollars out of here and the fragmentation of ownership.

317
00:22:49,280 --> 00:22:53,520
And if you think about it, the ability to streamline backend services is quite powerful here.

318
00:22:53,760 --> 00:22:55,120
Once again, it's a digital product.

319
00:22:55,440 --> 00:23:02,080
So there's companies like Maple Media in LA, Benning Spoons out of Italy, Mosaic, which is like an IC company in New York.

320
00:23:02,400 --> 00:23:08,960
They're all doing it and getting to some pretty good scale, lots of profitability, but it isn't where we expect it to be in three to five years, right?

321
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:15,920
So I'm sure you guys have all seen some rollouts that have happened with Amazon, they raised billions and they did it within about a span of like six months, absolutely wild.

322
00:23:16,160 --> 00:23:20,640
Now, that sector was probably well oversold and it became really competitive.

323
00:23:20,960 --> 00:23:23,920
And then I think a lot of those businesses are now starting to trend backwards.

324
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:25,760
They kind of rode the e commerce boom.

325
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:30,160
So that's probably a bad example, but it's an example of investors tossing money after a roll-up strategy.

326
00:23:30,560 --> 00:23:33,200
And we haven't seen that in the consumer subscription space yet.

327
00:23:33,360 --> 00:23:35,680
And so I've seen a lot of people do SaaS investing.

328
00:23:35,760 --> 00:23:38,320
We definitely have some rockstars that do CSS investing.

329
00:23:38,720 --> 00:23:43,040
But I think people are going to wake up to the dollars that are here and how profitable these businesses can be.

330
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:46,320
So I listed a couple of the names that I think have a really big chance.

331
00:23:46,480 --> 00:23:47,440
And there's plenty more, right?

332
00:23:47,600 --> 00:23:51,040
There's Reflectly out of Europe that's coming in, Apex out of Boston.

333
00:23:51,280 --> 00:23:53,760
There's a lot of names that are starting to pick up to come after this sector.

334
00:23:54,080 --> 00:23:58,960
Do you think part of that is on the banking side, not being able to price the risk for debt to buy these things?

335
00:23:59,280 --> 00:23:59,840
Are there macro?

336
00:24:00,240 --> 00:24:03,440
Because it's like, yeah, what's the difference between an an app and a car wash?

337
00:24:03,520 --> 00:24:04,480
Well, there's quite a few.

338
00:24:05,200 --> 00:24:05,760
Is that the reason?

339
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:09,360
Is it a microeconomic thing where sellers don't want to sell or buyers don't want to buy?

340
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:15,200
Or is it a macroeconomic thing where it's just like the current state of interest rates and compressions and whatnot don't support it?

341
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:17,360
I'd probably break it into a couple of categories.

342
00:24:17,600 --> 00:24:19,440
I think debt is a little more challenging here, right?

343
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:22,480
Their debt has now started to come for SaaS, like B2B SaaS software.

344
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:25,600
You're seeing private equity get pretty decent deadlines to do this.

345
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:32,560
I think you'll be able to see that with CSS, but you need to have a decent scale, free to find a way to profitability to get a lender to take a look at it.

346
00:24:32,880 --> 00:24:35,920
We've definitely seen some lenders that won't back transactions that we've done.

347
00:24:36,240 --> 00:24:40,080
Are they getting as favorable leverages as they would in like a SaaS business?

348
00:24:40,240 --> 00:24:42,720
You're not, but you also shouldn't take as much leverage.

349
00:24:42,960 --> 00:24:45,120
Favorable leverage here, I would actually change the definition.

350
00:24:45,360 --> 00:24:49,360
And SaaS, right, getting four to five times SaaS EBITDA isn't bad.

351
00:24:49,600 --> 00:24:52,960
I would recommend getting like two to three here in Consumers, so you have a little bit more leeway.

352
00:24:53,120 --> 00:24:54,720
You don't want to get into trouble on the debt side here.

353
00:24:55,200 --> 00:24:59,440
That sounds like just admitting to the unknowns that CSS is a little bit still an open question.

354
00:24:59,760 --> 00:25:01,200
Well, I wouldn't say it's an open question.

355
00:25:01,360 --> 00:25:02,880
It just has different characteristics, right?

356
00:25:03,120 --> 00:25:07,200
B two B SaaS, you can consistently say you'll have with really good B two B SaaS, right?

357
00:25:07,440 --> 00:25:08,560
90% gross retention.

358
00:25:08,720 --> 00:25:10,080
People look for much higher rates.

359
00:25:10,400 --> 00:25:14,800
And CSS, you look at 70% is a really good first year annual retention rate.

360
00:25:14,960 --> 00:25:18,800
So then mathematically, you shouldn't take on that much debt because you don't have that much cash flow guarantee.

361
00:25:18,880 --> 00:25:19,680
It's a mathematical equation.

362
00:25:20,240 --> 00:25:24,480
You're much more exposed to the acquisition environment, which tends to be more volatile.

363
00:25:24,800 --> 00:25:29,120
Yeah, and I think the idea is apps have been priced all over the board in the last two or three years.

364
00:25:29,280 --> 00:25:32,880
There's enough data points out there now that I think people will be able to price them much more accurately.

365
00:25:33,040 --> 00:25:36,400
And listen, my job is to ruin that pricing by selling my clients at the highest price.

366
00:25:36,560 --> 00:25:41,440
There'll be an average, and the average is where people will look at and try to decide if there's a purchase to be made.

367
00:25:41,920 --> 00:25:48,880
And so there's a lot of streamlining you can do in the back office around marketing, content creation, finance, and then you can also start doing bundling.

368
00:25:49,040 --> 00:25:53,360
So it actually makes sense to partner a few of these apps together, and you can't do that unless you truly own it.

369
00:25:53,440 --> 00:25:56,480
You know, there's companies like PALTA that's doing some really cool stuff in health and fitness.

370
00:25:56,800 --> 00:25:58,720
They're bundling about the health and fitness offerings together.

371
00:25:59,200 --> 00:26:05,680
If you look at Match, a publicly traded company, they just bundle dating and they don't care who you are or who you want to date or who you want to fall in love with.

372
00:26:05,840 --> 00:26:06,720
They've got a product for you.

373
00:26:07,040 --> 00:26:08,480
So you could cover everything in that space.

374
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:12,560
You could kind of say they're also Berkshire Hathaway of dating in the app store these days.

375
00:26:13,040 --> 00:26:14,240
So I do think there'll be a lot more.

376
00:26:14,400 --> 00:26:16,880
There'll be stuff that happens in fitness, there'll be stuff that happens in health.

377
00:26:17,200 --> 00:26:18,880
There'll definitely be stuff that happens in education.

378
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:20,320
Chegg is a really good example of that.

379
00:26:20,480 --> 00:26:23,360
When they bought Mathway, they'll definitely do some more stuff on that front.

380
00:26:23,600 --> 00:26:25,040
So I say stay tuned to this one.

381
00:26:25,200 --> 00:26:26,720
We think there'll be some really big outcomes here.

382
00:26:27,120 --> 00:26:30,400
And there have been a lot of MA in this space.

383
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:34,960
Any particular ones you wanted to call out of interesting deals that have happened recently?

384
00:26:35,440 --> 00:26:38,000
The biggest one that I would point to is Benning Spoons, just a real.

385
00:26:38,400 --> 00:26:40,320
really big deal with some Italian banks.

386
00:26:40,400 --> 00:26:42,160
They bought on about 600 million of debt.

387
00:26:42,240 --> 00:26:44,560
So back to our debt question, Jacob, that's a big check.

388
00:26:44,800 --> 00:26:48,400
And then we went out and bought Evernote, which is a really famous US app.

389
00:26:48,800 --> 00:26:52,800
It was a good business, had kind of maybe been left by the wayside, but rumored that they bought that.

390
00:26:52,960 --> 00:26:55,360
Won't disclose anything, but well north of 100 million dollars.

391
00:26:55,840 --> 00:26:57,120
It's a big business to buy.

392
00:26:57,600 --> 00:26:58,560
So I think that's one.

393
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:01,200
I think there will be a few more that start coming out.

394
00:27:01,440 --> 00:27:08,880
If you look at what Francisco Partners just did with the Weather Channel, people don't understand that, but there's a paid subscription feature in the Weather Channel that's wildly profitable.

395
00:27:09,280 --> 00:27:15,040
And Francisco Partners did the same thing with My Fitness Pal, which is once again wildly profitable and it's a free app.

396
00:27:15,600 --> 00:27:20,400
So I think you just need more people to start to think through that, right, and understand the economics and then the cash flows.

397
00:27:20,480 --> 00:27:22,560
And I think you'll see some more and more big outcomes here.

398
00:27:22,880 --> 00:27:24,400
You said a lot of people don't understand it.

399
00:27:24,560 --> 00:27:25,280
I don't understand it.

400
00:27:25,520 --> 00:27:27,440
So IBM bought the Weather Channel.

401
00:27:27,680 --> 00:27:29,520
It was like a subsidiary of IBM.

402
00:27:29,760 --> 00:27:34,240
And then that just got spun out of IBM to a private equity.

403
00:27:34,560 --> 00:27:34,960
Like why?

404
00:27:35,520 --> 00:27:38,560
And do you know any more about why and how all that happened?

405
00:27:38,960 --> 00:27:39,520
Their goal, right?

